{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/window/census-dry\\\")\nviews=sqlite3.connect((root/\\\"views.sqlite\\\").as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\narchive=sqlite3.connect((root/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\").as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nm=json.loads(Path(\\\"expected-links.json\\\").read_text())\nscope={int(k.split(\\\":\\\")[0]) for k,v in m.items() if v[\\\"class\\\"]==\\\"W\\\"}\nfound={}\nfor seq,r in views.execute(\\\"select seq,record_json from link_selections where status=\\\\x27proposed\\\\x27\\\"):\n if seq not in scope: continue\n calls=[c for c in json.loads(r)[\\\"calls\\\"] if c[\\\"stage\\\"]==\\\"reads\\\"]\n assert len(calls)==1\n c=calls[0]\n req=json.loads(archive.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(c[\\\"call_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])\n cstate=req[\\\"state\\\"].splitlines()\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",seq, c[\\\"request_sha256\\\"][:12], cstate[0], \\\"\\\\n\\\",next(l for l in cstate if l.startswith(\\\"Its evidence:\\\")))\n for qid,question in req[\\\"questions\\\"].items():\n  view=qid.split(\\\"__\\\")[1]\n  key=f\\\"{seq}:{view}\\\"\n  if m[key][\\\"class\\\"]\"'!=\"W\": continue\n  instr=question[\"instructions\"].splitlines()\n  txt=\"'\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in instr if l.startswith(\\\"    Evidence:\\\"))\n  prior=found.get(view)\n  found[view]=txt\n  print(view,instr[1],\\\"--\\\",(\\\"same evidence as preceding entry for \\\"+view) if prior==txt else txt)\nprint(\\\"\\\\nW requests reviewed\\\",sum(v[\\\"class\\\"]==\\\"W\\\" for v in m.values()))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot=Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/window/census-dry\\\")\nviews=sqlite3.connect((root/\\\"views.sqlite\\\").as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\narchive=sqlite3.connect((root/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\").as_uri()+\\\"?mode=ro&immutable=1\\\",uri=True)\nm=json.loads(Path(\\\"expected-links.json\\\").read_text())\nscope={int(k.split(\\\":\\\")[0]) for k,v in m.items() if v[\\\"class\\\"]==\\\"W\\\"}\nfound={}\nfor seq,r in views.execute(\\\"select seq,record_json from link_selections where status=\\\\x27proposed\\\\x27\\\"):\n if seq not in scope: continue\n calls=[c for c in json.loads(r)[\\\"calls\\\"] if c[\\\"stage\\\"]==\\\"reads\\\"]\n assert len(calls)==1\n c=calls[0]\n req=json.loads(archive.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(c[\\\"call_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])\n cstate=req[\\\"state\\\"].splitlines()\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",seq, c[\\\"request_sha256\\\"][:12], cstate[0], \\\"\\\\n\\\",next(l for l in cstate if l.startswith(\\\"Its evidence:\\\")))\n for qid,question in req[\\\"questions\\\"].items():\n  view=qid.split(\\\"__\\\")[1]\n  key=f\\\"{seq}:{view}\\\"\n  if m[key][\\\"class\\\"]\"'!=\"W\": continue\n  instr=question[\"instructions\"].splitlines()\n  txt=\"'\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in instr if l.startswith(\\\"    Evidence:\\\"))\n  prior=found.get(view)\n  found[view]=txt\n  print(view,instr[1],\\\"--\\\",(\\\"same evidence as preceding entry for \\\"+view) if prior==txt else txt)\nprint(\\\"\\\\nW requests reviewed\\\",sum(v[\\\"class\\\"]==\\\"W\\\" for v in m.values()))'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 206 8284d34ecdfc CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA supply commitments\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? \n Its evidence: [247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-13] NVIDIA, by contrast, carries $119 billion in supply commitments, tying its fortunes to hyperscaler order books .\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? --     Evidence: [NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01] This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates a\n    Evidence: [qz.com 2026-07-01] Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? --     Evidence: [moomoo.com 2026-07-01] On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\n    Evidence: [moomoo.com 2026-07-01] On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\n\n 746 f04bc966b522 CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 gross margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027? \n Its evidence: [trefis.com ] Gross margin, guided to the mid-seventies for fiscal 2027\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v1\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v2\n\n 821 75c7597c7548 CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? \n Its evidence: [finance.yahoo.com ] Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya. | [finance.yahoo.com ] \"Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\" Arya wrote.\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v1\nv14 v14 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? --     Evidence: [Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg ] The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\n    Evidence: [es.tradingview.com ] The bank noted that AI laboratories now account for roughly 20% of Nvidia’s demand, highlighting how enterprise AI adoption is expanding beyond hyperscale cloud providers.\nv15 v15 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA supply commitments\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? --     Evidence: [247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-13] NVIDIA, by contrast, carries $119 billion in supply commitments, tying its fortunes to hyperscaler order books .\n    Evidence: [247wallst.com 2026-07-14] If EPS scales from $8 toward $12 to $14 over five years (plausible given $96.58 billion FY26 free cash flow and $119 billion in supply commitments), a 30x multiple gets you there.\nv16 v16 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027? --     Evidence: [trefis.com, quoting NVIDIA management ] opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before.\n    Evidence: [trefis.com, quoting NVIDIA management ] visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\nv17 v17 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2028 total revenue growth\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2028? --     Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) ] they also predict growth slowing to a 41% revenue increase for fiscal 2028.\n    Evidence: [Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool ] According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v2\nv21 v21 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027? --     Evidence: [Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool ] By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\n    Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-27] With Alphabet poised to significantly raise its spending this year, and possibly in the future as well, due to the huge backlog that it is sitting on, it is likely to purchase more of Nvidia's AI chip systems. So, there is a solid chance of an improvement in Nvidia's growth rate.\nv28 v28 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 gross margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027? --     Evidence: [trefis.com ] Gross margin, guided to the mid-seventies for fiscal 2027\n    Evidence: [Tom's Hardware ] There has been some disturbing news that prices for RTX 50-series GPUs jumped by 30% in South Korea as wafer costs from TSMC have increased, and GDDR7 modules push past $20.\nv8 v8 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027? --     Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) ] Intel has led here for years and holds more than 59% of total CPU market share\n    Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-05] AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\nv9 v9 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? --     Evidence: [tradingview.com ] During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Evidence: [NVIDIA 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\n\n 1029 47a7dbd8349b CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA’s Poolside AI-technology licensing payment commitment reported in the FY2027 annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA’s committed payment to license Poolside’s AI technology reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? \n Its evidence: [nationalcioreview.com ] The company will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v1\nv14 v14 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v14\nv16 v16 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v16\nv17 v17 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2028 total revenue growth\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2028? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v17\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v2\nv21 v21 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v21\nv28 v28 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 gross margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v28\nv29 v29 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? --     Evidence: [finance.yahoo.com ] Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n    Evidence: [finance.yahoo.com ] \"Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\" Arya wrote.\nv8 v8 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v8\nv9 v9 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v9\n\n 1102 f5dae829b48c CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA FY2028 gross margin\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028? \n Its evidence: [finance.yahoo.com 2026-08-26] Gross margins, which were 75% in the second quarter, are projected to drop to 74% in Q3, bottom out at 71–72% in Q4 FY27, and settle at 72–73% in FY28 after Nvidia's price increases take effect in Q1. | [Colette Kress, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com ] Many of you have expressed concerns regarding our gross margins as component costs have risen significantly. As you are already aware, we are experiencing extreme pricing conditions in memory... As a result, we are resetting expectations today, | [Colette Kress, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com ] We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1,\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v1\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v2\nv29 v29 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v29\nv32 v32 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA’s Poolside AI-technology licensing payment commitment reported in the FY2027 annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA’s committed payment to license Poolside’s AI technology reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? --     Evidence: [nationalcioreview.com ] The company will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology\n\n 1238 66efa39f1070 CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028? \n Its evidence: [fool.com (analyst) ] Analysts see revenue reaching about $390 billion in 2027 and more than $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24% to 25%. | [fool.com (analyst), reporting NVIDIA management's outlook ] Management's outlook is even stronger: about 70% growth in fiscal 2028, which could put revenue near $670 billion.\nv29 v29 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v29\nv32 v32 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA’s Poolside AI-technology licensing payment commitment reported in the FY2027 annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA’s committed payment to license Poolside’s AI technology reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? --     Evidence: [nationalcioreview.com ] The company will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology\n    Evidence: [Time ] More recently, it struck a $6 billion deal with Poolside, to develop an American open alternative.\n\n 1262 2016a390ee72 CANDIDATE (asset, object NVDA, focus \"NVIDIA's investment commitment to MediaTek through convertible bonds\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's committed investment in MediaTek through convertible bonds reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? \n Its evidence: [finance.yahoo.com 2026-08-31] Nvidia announced on Monday that it is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds, deepening a partnership with the Taiwanese chipmaker that spans AI data center infrastructure, consumer PCs, and automotive platforms.\nv1 v1 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA's credit-support obligations under the AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed under its AI-cloud model by the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v1\nv14 v14 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v14\nv15 v15 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA supply commitments\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v15\nv16 v16 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v16\nv17 v17 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2028 total revenue growth\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2028? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v17\nv2 v2 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's credit support and capacity backstop obligations to customers and cloud providers\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, how much will its commitments for customer and cloud-provider credit support, guarantees, and capacity backstops reach? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v2\nv21 v21 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v21\nv28 v28 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 gross margin\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v28\nv32 v32 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of NVIDIA’s Poolside AI-technology licensing payment commitment reported in the FY2027 annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA’s committed payment to license Poolside’s AI technology reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v32\nv33 v33 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2028 gross margin\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028? --     Evidence: [finance.yahoo.com 2026-08-26] Gross margins, which were 75% in the second quarter, are projected to drop to 74% in Q3, bottom out at 71–72% in Q4 FY27, and settle at 72–73% in FY28 after Nvidia's price increases take effect in Q1.\n    Evidence: [Colette Kress, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com ] Many of you have expressed concerns regarding our gross margins as component costs have risen significantly. As you are already aware, we are experiencing extreme pricing conditions in memory... As a result, we are resetting expectations today,\nv35 v35 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\", FY2028, due 2028-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028? --     Evidence: [fool.com (analyst) ] Analysts see revenue reaching about $390 billion in 2027 and more than $550 billion in 2028, with annual sales growth of roughly 24% to 25%.\n    Evidence: [fool.com (analyst), reporting NVIDIA management's outlook ] Management's outlook is even stronger: about 70% growth in fiscal 2028, which could put revenue near $670 billion.\nv8 v8 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA stand-alone CPU sales\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v8\nv9 v9 (open, NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027? -- same evidence as preceding entry for v9\n\nW requests reviewed 43\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
